FUSE over a Custom Transport
============================

This document describes fuse_session_custom_io(), which lets a session speak
the FUSE protocol over a file descriptor the caller provides instead of over
/dev/fuse, and the validation such a caller has to do.

The example that ships with libfuse, example/hello_ll_uds.c, uses an AF_UNIX
socket, which is where the shorthand "UDS" comes from. The API itself does not
care what the descriptor is: a socket, a pipe pair, anything a read() and a
writev() work on.


Disabled by Default
-------------------

fuse_session_custom_io() returns -ENOTSUP and logs a message unless libfuse
was built with:

    meson setup build -Denable-custom-io=true

The hello_ll_uds example is built only with that option, and the test that
drives it skips without it. The reason is the trust model described below,
not the transport itself.

The symbols remain exported in every build, so an application that calls
fuse_session_custom_io() keeps linking and loading; it is refused at run time.


How It Works
------------

A normal session gets its descriptor from the kernel and the kernel is the
peer at the other end:

    fuse_session_new()
         |
         v
    fuse_session_mount()  --> open /dev/fuse, mount()
         |
         v
    fuse_session_loop()   --> read()/writev() on the /dev/fuse fd
                              peer: the kernel FUSE module

A custom io session never mounts anything. The caller connects the descriptor
itself and hands it, plus the io callbacks, to the session:

    fuse_session_new()
         |
         v
    caller creates fd     --> socket(), connect()/accept(), ...
         |
         v
    fuse_session_custom_io(se, &io, sizeof(io), fd)
         |
         v
    fuse_session_loop()   --> io->read()/io->writev() on that fd
                              peer: whatever is at the other end

From FUSE_INIT onwards the protocol is identical. The peer sends requests in
the same wire format the kernel would, and the session replies the same way.


The io Callbacks
----------------

struct fuse_custom_io has five members. read and writev are mandatory:
fuse_session_custom_io() returns -EINVAL without them, because libfuse cannot
fall back to plain read()/writev() on a descriptor it does not own.

1. **read(fd, buf, buf_len, userdata)**: must deliver exactly one request per
   call. buf_len is the session buffer size. A return value smaller than
   sizeof(struct fuse_in_header) ends the session loop with -EIO and the
   message "short read on fuse device", which is also what a peer closing the
   connection looks like.

2. **writev(fd, iov, count, userdata)**: must write every iovec.

3. **splice_send** and **splice_receive**: optional. Without them the session
   does not offer FUSE_CAP_SPLICE_WRITE, FUSE_CAP_SPLICE_MOVE and
   FUSE_CAP_SPLICE_READ, so replies and requests are copied instead.

4. **clone_fd**: optional, called by the multi-threaded loop to get a
   descriptor per worker. Without it every worker shares the one descriptor.

The descriptor is closed by fuse_session_destroy().


What a Custom io Session Does Not Have
--------------------------------------

1. **No mountpoint.** fuse_session_mount() and fusermount3 are not involved,
   so none of the mount options, the setuid helper or /etc/mtab bookkeeping
   apply.

2. **No kernel.** FUSE_INIT arrives from the peer, so protocol version and
   feature negotiation are whatever the peer asks for.

3. **No io_uring.** fuse_session_custom_io() clears se->uring.enable: the ring
   is issued against a /dev/fuse descriptor and would bypass the io callbacks
   entirely. A session reports this on its own stderr as
   "FUSE_INIT: io_uring=off:custom_io".

4. **No trustworthy credentials.** The uid, gid and pid that fuse_req_ctx()
   returns are the ones the peer wrote into fuse_in_header. The allow_root
   option compares that same uid against the session owner, so it states what
   the peer claims, not who the peer is.


The Trust Model: Unverified Opcodes
-----------------------------------

libfuse was written against one producer, the Linux kernel, which does not
need to validate the requests it sends because it builds them itself. A
kernel-built request always has:

- a fuse_in_header whose len matches the bytes written,
- the per-opcode header the opcode calls for,
- name arguments NUL terminated inside the message,
- count and size fields describing data that is actually present.

libfuse repeats almost none of these checks. What it does check:

- FUSE_INIT and CUSE_INIT: enough bytes for the major/minor fields, at
  dispatch time, otherwise EPROTO.
- FUSE_WRITE handled by op.write_buf: fuse_write_in.size against the bytes
  received, otherwise EIO.
- FUSE_NOTIFY_REPLY: the same check.
- The extension block: FUSE_EXT_SIZE(total_extlen) against the buffer length.

Every other opcode is taken at face value, in three classes:

1. **Entry counts.** fuse_batch_forget_in.count bounds the loop over the
   fuse_forget_one array that follows it. Nothing compares it to the bytes
   that arrived, so an inflated count reads count * 16 bytes past the array
   and hands them to op.forget or op.forget_multi as inode numbers and
   lookup counts.

2. **Payload lengths.** FUSE_SETXATTR passes fuse_setxattr_in.size to
   op.setxattr as the length of the value, without checking that many bytes
   were received.

3. **Strings.** Name arguments are handed to the filesystem as a const char *
   and are expected to be terminated. FUSE_SYMLINK, FUSE_RENAME, FUSE_RENAME2
   and FUSE_SETXATTR locate their second argument at name + strlen(name) + 1.
   A name without a NUL runs off the end of the receive buffer.

A malformed request therefore reads memory outside the receive buffer. The
usual outcome is a crash of the filesystem process; the batch forget case also
feeds the out-of-bounds words into the filesystem's inode bookkeeping.

**A custom io peer owes the session the validation the kernel would have made
unnecessary.** The read callback is the place for it, because that is the last
point before libfuse parses the bytes. At minimum, per message:

- reject a fuse_in_header whose len is below sizeof(struct fuse_in_header) or
  above the buffer size, and deliver exactly len bytes;
- reject an opcode whose per-opcode header does not fit in what arrived;
- check every count and size field against the remaining bytes;
- check that every name argument is NUL terminated within the message.

A peer that forwards requests it did not build, or that accepts connections
from anything but itself, has to treat this as the security boundary it is.


Example
-------

example/hello_ll_uds.c serves the protocol on an AF_UNIX socket:

    se = fuse_session_new(&args, &hello_ll_oper, sizeof(hello_ll_oper), NULL);
    cfd = create_socket(socket_path);   /* socket, bind, listen, accept */
    fuse_session_custom_io(se, &io, sizeof(io), cfd);
    fuse_session_loop(se);

Its stream_read() reads the header, takes the length from it and reads the
rest of the message, which is the framing the read callback contract asks for
but none of the validation the section above asks for. It is an example of the
API, not a model for a peer that is not trusted.


